WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.073
Deliberate practice requires focused attention.

2
00:00:02.073 --> 00:00:10.782
The learner is not just completing repetitions; they are watching for a specific feature of performance and trying to improve it.

3
00:00:10.782 --> 00:00:12.441
That attention is limited.

4
00:00:12.441 --> 00:00:22.808
Distractions, unclear goals, excessive complexity, and long unfocused sessions all make practice less effective because the learner cannot process the feedback and correction that matter.

5
00:00:22.808 --> 00:00:27.369
The strongest practice often happens near the edge of current ability.

6
00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:33.175
The task should be challenging enough to stretch skill, reveal gaps, and require effort.

7
00:00:33.175 --> 00:00:40.225
It should not be so hard that the learner becomes overwhelmed and cannot tell what to change.

8
00:00:40.225 --> 00:00:41.883
Productive struggle creates information.

9
00:00:41.883 --> 00:00:43.127
Overload creates noise.

10
00:00:43.127 --> 00:00:47.274
Good practice design adjusts the difficulty so effort stays useful.

11
00:00:47.274 --> 00:00:50.592
Cognitive load matters because working memory has limits.

12
00:00:50.592 --> 00:01:00.544
If a learner is trying to manage too many new elements at once, performance may break down for reasons unrelated to the target skill.

13
00:01:00.544 --> 00:01:05.106
Short focused sessions can be more valuable than long unfocused sessions.

14
00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:12.985
Rest between attempts helps learners consolidate, reset attention, and return with enough mental energy to correct the next attempt.

15
00:01:12.985 --> 00:01:18.790
Deliberate practice is mentally demanding, but it should not become unsafe or unsustainable intensity.

16
00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.693
More strain is not automatically better learning.

17
00:01:21.693 --> 00:01:25.011
A disciplined practice system uses difficulty with care.

18
00:01:25.011 --> 00:01:37.451
It protects attention, reduces unnecessary distractions, sets a clear target, and treats recovery as part of performance development rather than a sign that the learner is not trying hard enough.
